WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.500
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Ward Farnsworth, who is dean of the University

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.300
of Texas Law School and also author of the
Socratic Method A Practitioner's Handbook.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.129
It's so great to have you on today.

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:13.339
I really appreciate it.

00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:14.879
Speaker 3: Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:15.879
Good to be here.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:21.010
Speaker 1: So I talk about the Socratic method
with my audience frequently and I think may

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:25.490
be the best place to start would be when we
say Socratic.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.120
In that word, we're talking about Socrates.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.680
So maybe we can start with the origins of
why this is.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.010
This is a method attributed to Socrates and
what it is.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.240
Speaker 3: Sure.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.240
Well.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.190
When people talk about the Socratic method
and Socrates, what they usually mean is the

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.360
style of dialog and discourse and thinking
that you see displayed by Socrates in the

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.070
dialogs of Plato.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.910
We don't really know much about the real Socrates,
whether that's how we talked.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.560
We know that's how the literary Socrates talked.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.989
And that's the model for the Socratic method.

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:05.250
Speaker 1: And the model is characterized
by questions, but particular types of questions

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.860
meant to elicit a certain type of thinking.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.650
Can you talk us through that a little bit?

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:15.520
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think the Socratic method
is the greatest tool there is for critical

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.330
thinking and for testing people's views.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:23.080
It has a lot of features and they're hard
to summarize quickly, but usually with Socrates

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.650
is trying to do is work with the beliefs of
the person he's talking to.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.390
Society doesn't usually say you're wrong,
he says.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.920
Can we agree on this?

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.170
Can we agree on that?

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:39.851
Well, but but if if we agree on those things,
then what about this other thing that you

00:01:39.851 --> 00:01:40.851
said?

00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:43.090
It seems like there's a tension there or an
issue.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.869
So he basically talks people into contradiction
with themselves, which is just, to me, a distinctly

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:51.610
effective way of working with people who don't
agree with.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:57.670
Speaker 1: One of the things that sometimes
is frustrating in the modern landscape is

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:04.170
you might get someone to contradict themselves,
but they seem unable to recognize that they've

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.170
contradicted themselves.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.560
And so, for example, we see this frequently
when I will talk to people who say that Trump

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.040
is actually the president and it's Joe Biden's
fault that gas prices are high.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.650
And it's like, Well, if Joe Biden's not really
the president, how could it be his fault that

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.120
gas prices are high?

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.090
That's sort of the contradiction.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:27.940
But sometimes people seem to lack an ability
to even understand the contradiction.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.040
How would the Socratic method address that?

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:36.900
Speaker 3: Well, first of all, if they don't
think there's a contradiction, then and I'm

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.020
the one using the method, then my feeling
is that's on me.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.930
My questions have not been skillful enough.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.930
Or maybe I'm wrong.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:43.930
Maybe there isn't a contradiction.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.770
They don't think there is one because it looks
like there's one to you.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:50.580
But what they mean when they say Donald Trump
is really president isn't what you think they

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.580
mean.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.660
They mean this and you think they mean that.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.650
And so there's something additional about
their meaning that you've got to get to the

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.140
bottom of that.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.490
I just think the right way to think about
this is always ask whether if you're not making

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.680
headway, it's really on them.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.620
Take responsibility and see if you can be
more empathetic and try to understand what

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.239
they mean or what they're thinking.

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:14.990
That's that's an important part of the Socratic
method is you're trying to get people to come

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.800
around to your way of thinking.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.910
And before you can do that, you kind of got
to come around to their way of thinking or

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:21.250
really understand it.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.590
And not just not just like what passes for
thoughts, not what, not what passes for thought

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.580
in the minds of these people you think are
idiots.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:31.910
But but how they actually look at the world
and why it makes sense to them.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:37.040
And once you really get that, it's much easier
from that standpoint to then bring them around

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.170
to something that's more like the way you
look at things.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:45.020
Speaker 1: One of the things that seems important
in the Socratic method is a sort of humility

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:51.690
about our own beliefs, and it seems that the
approach of asking questions can be humble

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.470
in a sense, because you're you're really trying
to learn more about what the other person

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.129
believes, even if your ultimate goal is that
you suspect they're wrong and you want to

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:02.720
change their mind at the same time.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.659
There's sometimes the assumption that it's
more persuasive to appear more confident in

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:09.950
what you believe.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:14.950
So is there a balance that needs to be struck
or is it just not true that it's more persuasive

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.019
to be confident?

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:20.939
Speaker 3: Well, I suppose it's hard to generalize,
I'm sure there are people who are very persuaded

00:04:20.939 --> 00:04:26.159
by confidence and not by humility, but the
Socratic method, in any event, is is founded

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:27.159
on humility.

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:31.319
I mean, I know the Socratic method sounds
fundamentally like a technique for questioning

00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:33.730
other people and for trying to bring them
around in it.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:35.039
It's very powerful for that.

00:04:35.039 --> 00:04:39.770
But to me, first and foremost, it's a style
of thought that if you're if you really care

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.660
about these things, you try to use yourself.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.749
So you're also you're questioning what you
think and you're wondering if maybe you're

00:04:44.749 --> 00:04:45.809
wrong.

00:04:45.809 --> 00:04:50.770
And so when you're talking to people, you're
trying to help questioning them to see things

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:51.770
your way.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.979
But you're also alert to the idea that maybe
you have something to learn from them.

00:04:55.979 --> 00:05:00.159
And I think that that one byproduct of that
approach is that if you're humble in that

00:05:00.159 --> 00:05:02.469
way, you're more likely to make headway with
others.

00:05:02.469 --> 00:05:06.520
But you're also more likely to make headway
in seeing your own short sightedness, which

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.810
is of course, something we should all want.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.949
Speaker 1: Can can we talk through in practice?

00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:17.819
How this might sort of appear in if it's happening
at the Thanksgiving table is often the one

00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:18.819
people call in.

00:05:18.819 --> 00:05:20.499
They say, I know my uncle believes that.

00:05:20.499 --> 00:05:23.249
I mean, we could just stick with my uncle
believes Trump won.

00:05:23.249 --> 00:05:24.539
He just believes it was stolen.

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:27.069
He believes Joe Biden stole it and he's going
to bring it up.

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:32.240
And he's going to say, Can you guys believe
what Joe Biden was able to get away with?

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.819
And people want sort of a framework with which
they can without upsetting people without

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:40.129
ruining relationships?

00:05:40.129 --> 00:05:42.569
Start a gentle Socratic inquiry.

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:46.639
What types of questions might we we ask in
that situation?

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:47.849
Speaker 3: Well, good question.

00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:52.279
I mean, the first question is, you know, is
it worthwhile the Thanksgiving table to try

00:05:52.279 --> 00:05:53.539
to chase this down?

00:05:53.539 --> 00:05:55.350
Speaker 1: Let's assume it is.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.350
Let's assume it is.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.350
This person will
Speaker 3: assume it is.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:01.039
But I just mean, you can be Socratic about
the value of using these methods.

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:02.039
Sure.

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:03.740
But but putting that to one side?

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:04.740
Yeah.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.279
I mean, to me, the goal was always, can you
find some common ground?

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:11.119
Can you find something you agree on that that
you can then build from to saying something

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:15.809
like, you know, look what, here's what I'm
interested in.

00:06:15.809 --> 00:06:22.319
What would you be willing to talk about as
evidence that the election was stolen or what's

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:24.919
or what are you going to count as evidence
of anything?

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:28.220
I mean, like, let's just take something that's
less controversial and just see if we can

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.220
agree on that.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.280
How do you know that somebody else won the
presidential election?

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.280
Let's talk.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.530
Let's talk about the election we can agree
on.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:34.530
Right?

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:35.530
How do you know who won that election?

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:36.530
I'm just interested.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.419
I mean, I'm really wondering, how do you how
do you think about that?

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:41.889
Because in a sense, we don't know how we know
what we read in the paper.

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:48.129
So what makes you so sure that somebody like
that the Donald Trump won the first election

00:06:48.129 --> 00:06:51.479
and let them explain that for a while and
you say, OK, so that's interesting.

00:06:51.479 --> 00:06:54.929
So I guess what I'm wondering now is of those
things you mentioned, how many of those things

00:06:54.929 --> 00:07:00.639
are not also present in the election you are
worried about and trying to fight an election

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:04.349
you can agree on before you start talking
about election, you can agree on.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:07.679
I think it is a helpful approach, and I would
just use that approach in general to try to

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:12.749
find a case you can agree on and then talk
through what that case is about and then try

00:07:12.749 --> 00:07:18.309
to use what you've established in your in
your easy case to sort of gently ask about

00:07:18.309 --> 00:07:19.309
the hard case.

00:07:19.309 --> 00:07:20.309
Speaker 1: That's really interesting.

00:07:20.309 --> 00:07:24.749
It seems there is maybe in thinking about
the empathy component there as well.

00:07:24.749 --> 00:07:30.159
There might be an opportunity also to find
common ground in this conversation to say,

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:35.909
Do do are we on the same page that we both
want whoever really won to be the president,

00:07:35.909 --> 00:07:36.909
right?

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:39.439
Are we on the same page that that that's that's
ultimately what we want.

00:07:39.439 --> 00:07:43.469
We want to know who really won, but that person
should definitely be the president.

00:07:43.469 --> 00:07:44.899
It seems that might also be useful.

00:07:44.899 --> 00:07:45.899
Speaker 3: Definitely.

00:07:45.899 --> 00:07:48.970
But then, of course, and you know, so after
I think everybody would agree on that.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:50.399
It's a good thing to start with.

00:07:50.399 --> 00:07:53.810
And then and then you'd say, OK, another question
is what does it mean to say somebody really

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.810
won?

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:55.810
Yeah.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:56.810
Like, what do you mean?

00:07:56.810 --> 00:07:57.810
Somebody's got the most popular vote?

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.129
Or do you mean the person who won the Electoral
College?

00:08:00.129 --> 00:08:01.209
Forget about Trump for a minute.

00:08:01.209 --> 00:08:03.310
Just what do we mean in general?

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.749
And you keep trying to build agreements on
things once you have these agreements in place,

00:08:06.749 --> 00:08:08.249
you know everything, then.

00:08:08.249 --> 00:08:09.959
And you know, I still think that.

00:08:09.959 --> 00:08:14.979
I mean, in these conversations, of course,
you're always hopefully thinking, Look, maybe

00:08:14.979 --> 00:08:17.999
we're in something and you can say to the
person, Honestly, let me tell you if this

00:08:17.999 --> 00:08:19.620
election was stolen, I want to know.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.969
I mean, I don't want to live under a stolen
election.

00:08:21.969 --> 00:08:23.009
I'm sure you don't, either.

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:28.619
So if there's any evidence that this was stolen,
I certainly would be grateful to hear about

00:08:28.619 --> 00:08:29.619
it.

00:08:29.619 --> 00:08:30.619
And I think you can say that.

00:08:30.619 --> 00:08:32.330
I think anybody can say that and read it.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:37.930
Speaker 1: What about with one that can be
sometimes when the topic lends itself more

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:42.509
to the ideas of experts and who counts as
an expert?

00:08:42.509 --> 00:08:43.880
It can get much more dicey.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:49.680
So, for example, we could think about COVID
and vaccines, right where there's not only

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:58.170
a disagreement about facts, there is a disagreement
or misunderstanding about who is a valid authority

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.949
and about the differences between facts and
opinions.

00:09:01.949 --> 00:09:07.269
Oftentimes, what our opinions are put out
there, as if it is the type of statement that

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:11.360
counts as a fact when really it's only an
opinion, and it seems that it's a much tougher

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.500
hill to climb, seemingly armed only with the
Socratic method of questioning how.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:21.050
How might would the would the application
be different in such circumstances?

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:28.040
Speaker 3: Well, I don't think so, because
you can still ask questions about other situations

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.370
where people defer to experts, like you might
say.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.529
Let's talk about some are there, are there
any vaccines you do take?

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:42.630
Well, why do you take those, are there situations
where expert opinions are enough for you?

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.030
Because there are for me?

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:47.110
And I think actually most of us defer to experts
sometimes.

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:51.410
And so the question is, when can you fight
a case where the answer is yes?

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:52.770
And then what are the features of that case?

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:57.010
And then how do the features of that case
compare to the features of the case and the

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.029
case that's in controversy?

00:09:58.029 --> 00:10:04.200
I think that's a Socratic idea as you always
try to find something that's triggered move

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:08.800
over to an easier case or you can agree and
then establish the things you can agree on

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.480
because usually what's going on is in the
controversial case, everybody's judgment is

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.910
clouded by other features of it, by the politics
of it or the rooting interest that you have,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.613
like, almost like as a sports fan.

00:10:20.613 --> 00:10:23.990
And so you're trying to go find a case that
is not clouded by those things and establish

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:27.379
your principles there and then gently move
over into the case.

00:10:27.379 --> 00:10:28.459
That's giving everybody more trouble.

00:10:28.459 --> 00:10:32.639
Speaker 1: What about specifically, when were
this?

00:10:32.639 --> 00:10:35.509
Sometimes the struggle will be when people
will call me.

00:10:35.509 --> 00:10:42.279
I might present what are you know, what I
see as data and reports about a particular

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:43.279
issue?

00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:46.740
And someone will say, Well, my experience
is just different.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.740
So it's an anecdote.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:48.740
Right?

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:55.201
We're talking about anecdotes versus empiricism,
and it's very easy and reflexive to say, that's

00:10:55.201 --> 00:10:57.800
an anecdote and we need to just look at.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.720
We need to look at broader evidence, but people
are often pretty resistant to that.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:04.100
They'll say, No, I mean, listen, this is what
I experienced.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.920
And so this is real to me in some sense.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.790
What sort of advice do you have in that type
of situation?

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:13.250
Speaker 3: Well, I mean, I think first, just
just listen to people.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.069
I mean, nobody's gonna listen to you unless
they feel like you listen to them.

00:11:16.069 --> 00:11:20.470
So if your immediate reaction is obvious contempt
for their experience, then you're just not

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.589
going to be off to an effective start.

00:11:22.589 --> 00:11:25.050
But let's imagine that you've done that.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:30.160
And I think at that point, maybe the most
helpful way to reason is to ask, Well, how

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:36.519
do they feel when people make similar claims
that are anecdotal or about their own experience,

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:37.519
but who they don't agree with?

00:11:37.519 --> 00:11:40.480
I mean, you know, because that's the kind
of claim that people can't stand when it's

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.819
made by those on the other side.

00:11:41.819 --> 00:11:45.611
So trying to think of an example, I mean,
that's what I think in general is if you if

00:11:45.611 --> 00:11:50.819
you can flip the politics in a situation like,
imagine exactly what you're saying, but imagine

00:11:50.819 --> 00:11:55.120
somebody on the other side making that claim
what you know you can ask me, what would you

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:56.120
say to them?

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:57.699
What would you think of the strength of that
claim?

00:11:57.699 --> 00:11:58.699
I mean, wouldn't I?

00:11:58.699 --> 00:11:59.699
I'm not.

00:11:59.699 --> 00:12:01.699
I'm not trying to tell you how you feel, but
how do you think?

00:12:01.699 --> 00:12:02.699
How do you think you'd feel?

00:12:02.699 --> 00:12:03.769
And of course, it's always perfect.

00:12:03.769 --> 00:12:07.779
If you can think of an actual example and
say, Well, you know what you're saying?

00:12:07.779 --> 00:12:11.790
Reminds me of somebody I knew that you may
have heard about who actually made a very

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:16.259
similar claim, but except that they they love
everything you hate.

00:12:16.259 --> 00:12:19.319
And I just wonder, how would you work with
that person and say, Well, that person just

00:12:19.319 --> 00:12:20.319
wrote.

00:12:20.319 --> 00:12:21.699
Well, I know you think that, but they do.

00:12:21.699 --> 00:12:23.480
They don't think that's what would you know?

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.629
I guess, I guess we probably say to them is
their ideas about what counts as evidence

00:12:27.629 --> 00:12:30.240
don't seem very strong.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.160
And then maybe you've got a name wrote, I
mean, these are these are not conversations

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:38.720
that you know, you can expect, which you can
expect to turn somebody 180 degrees.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.302
The question is, can you make a little headway?

00:12:41.302 --> 00:12:46.129
